\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb112\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\sect\sectd\sbkpage\headery725\footery743\margtsxn1100\margbsxn940\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\fi50\ri427\sl244\slmult1\sb111\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 A good analogy\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 of inertia tuning is the "water hammer" effect\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 in old plumbing when you quickly\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 close a running faucet.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 (New plumbing has "trapped air shock absorbers" to reduce this effect.)\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Decelerating the column of water in the pipes by closing the valve causes a sharp pressure rise that literally "rattle the pipes".\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 This is the same situation as when the intake valve closes.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb2\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\ri427\sl244\slmult1\sb1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Inertia tuning is responsible for an increase in volumetric efficiency\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 and torque over a broad RPM\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 range.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 See Figure A.10. Inertia tuning is optimum when the rise in intake runner pressure is high over a broad range of the valve opening period, especially around intake valve closing.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 This usually occurs when In Tune Prs is highest in the RPM data test results.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 See Example 4.5.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb4\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s6\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\sl240\slmult1\f1\fs36\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Intake\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 Resonance\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw-2 Tuning\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\ri427\sl244\slmult1\sb237\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 Once the intake valve has closed, this pressure pulsation continues to be reflected back and forth in the intake runner until the intake valve opens and the process starts again.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 If a high pressure peak of this reflected wave is at the intake valve during\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 valve overlap, it can blow the burnt exhaust gasses out of the clearance volume into the exhaust.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 This results in additional gains in volumetric efficiency.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb1\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\ri427\sl244\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 However, if a vacuum peak arrives at the intake valve during overlap, additional exhaust gasses will be drawn into the intake runner.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 This\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 process\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 hurts\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 volumetric\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 efficiency\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 since\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 cylinder\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 must\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 first\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 draw\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 in that\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 "blown\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 back"\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 exhaust\expnd0\expndtw-2  \expnd0\expndtw0 from\expnd-1\expndtw-3  \expnd0\expndtw0 the\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 intake runner before it starts to induct the fresh, power producing air charge.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 The timing of this reflected wave to aid scavenging is called resonance tuning.\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\sl240\slmult1\sb2\f0\fs20\b0\i0\par\pard\plain\s1\nooverflow\nocwrap\lnbrkrule\li806\ri427\sl244\slmult1\f0\fs20\b0\expnd0\expndtw0\charscalex100 For typical runner lengths (6-20 inches) and RPMs (3000-10000 RPM), the resonant pressure wave is reflected several times between intake strokes.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Therefore, at some RPMs positive intake tuning occurs and negative tuning occurs at other RPMs. Resonance tuning is responsible for the "wiggle" seen in torque\expnd0\expndtw-1  \expnd0\expndtw0 curves for engines with high valve overlap.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 For engines with low overlap, resonant tuning has much less effect.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 Also, each time this wave is reflected it looses some strength, so at lower RPMs or for shorter runner lengths resonance tuning is negligible.\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 The Engine Analyzer Pro detailed tuning simulations will show\expnd8\expndtw40  \expnd0\expndtw0 these gains or losses in volumetric efficiency and performance for resonance tuning.\par
